.TH HYPRFETCH 1 "2026-10-23" "hyprfetch 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hyprfetch \- Hyprland-native fetch utility for system, session and rice state
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hyprfetch
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hyprfetch
prints the state of the machine and of the running Hyprland session next to a
distro logo, in the style of a classic fetch screenshot. It reports the
operating system, the hardware, the desktop theme, and the live compositor
state such as the active workspace, the focused window, the monitors, the
layer-shell surfaces and the running desktop daemons.
.PP
Modules that need Hyprland report why they are unavailable instead of
aborting the run, so the tool stays usable on a plain desktop.
.PP
The logo is chosen from
.I os-release
when
.B --logo auto
is used, which is the default. When the distribution is not known, the
generic Linux logo is used.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Read the configuration from
.I PATH
instead of the default location.
.TP
.B \-\-init\-config
Write a starter configuration to the default location. An existing file is
never overwritten.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-config
Print the default configuration to standard output and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Ignore the configuration file for this run.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Print plain key and value rows without the logo, the header or the colour
bars.
.TP
.B \-\-share
Use the share preset, which leads with the theme and the live session
state. It is meant for rice screenshots.
.TP
.B \-\-animated
Show a spinner while the modules are collected.
.TP
.B \-\-watch
Keep running and redraw the output in place. The screen is only cleared
when standard output is a terminal.
.TP
.BI \-\-watch\-interval " " \fISECONDS\fR
How long to wait between two refreshes of
.BR \-\-watch .
Defaults to 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-style " " \fISTYLE\fR
The layout to use:
.B split
places the logo next to the rows,
.B compact
drops the logo and prints one row per module, and
.B minimal
prints a bare
.IR key : value
list.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " " \fIMODE\fR
When to colour the output:
.B auto
follows the terminal and the
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable,
.B always
colours it and
.B never
turns it off.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-modules " " \fIMODULES\fR
A comma separated list of the modules to show. The options configured for
these modules in the configuration file are kept.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " " \fIPALETTE\fR
The colour palette:
.BR hypr ,
.BR mono ,
.B catppuccin
or
.BR dracula .
.TP
.BI \-\-logo " " \fIPRESET\fR
The logo to use. Besides the distribution logos there are
.B minimal
for the wordmark and
.B none
to hide the logo. Run
.B hyprfetch \-\-list\-logos
for the full list.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the collected modules as JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-diagnostics
Print the status of every module, including the ones that are
unavailable, and a summary of the counts.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-logos
Print the names of the available logos and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-modules
Print the names of the available modules and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-themes
Print the names of the available palettes and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short help text and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is read from
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hyprfetch/config.toml
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, and from
.I ~/.config/hyprfetch/config.toml
otherwise.
.PP
It holds the appearance, the logo, the order of the modules, the options of
each module and the command plugins. The order of the
.B [[modules]]
entries is the order they are printed in, and an entry may carry its own
options:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[[modules]]
name = "disk"
mounts = ["/", "/home"]

[[modules]]
name = "hypr-devices"
compact = false
.RE
.fi
.PP
The
.B mounts
option of the disk module lists the mount points it reports. The
.B compact
option of a module switches it between a single summary row and one row per
detail. The
.B list
option of the binds, layers, rules and shortcuts modules adds one row per
entry, and the
.B devices
option of the devices module does the same for the input devices.
.SH PLUGINS
A plugin runs a command and turns its output into rows, which lets a user
add information that
.B hyprfetch
does not know about:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[[plugins]]
name = "music"
command = "sh"
args = ["-c", "printf 'Track\\t%s\\n' \\"Lo-fi radio\\""]
timeout_ms = 1000
.RE
.fi
.PP
A single plain line becomes one row using the
.B label
or the
.B name
of the plugin.
.B Key<TAB>Value
and
.B Key=Value
lines become explicit rows. A plugin that fails or runs into its timeout
keeps the reason in the output.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The directory the configuration is read from.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Keeps the colour out of the output when
.B \-\-color auto
is used.
.TP
.B USER ", " USERNAME ", " HOSTNAME
Used for the session title.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hyprfetch
exits with 0 when it printed its output, and with a non zero status when it
could not run at all. A module that is unavailable does not change the
exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the default output:
.PP
.nf
.RS
hyprfetch
.RE
.fi
.PP
A screenshot for a rice:
.PP
.nf
.RS
hyprfetch --share
.RE
.fi
.PP
Only a few modules, without the logo:
.PP
.nf
.RS
hyprfetch --plain --modules os,kernel,memory,hyprland
.RE
.fi
.PP
Keep the session state on screen:
.PP
.nf
.RS
hyprfetch --watch --watch-interval 2
.RE
.fi
.PP
Check what a machine without Hyprland would report:
.PP
.nf
.RS
hyprfetch --diagnostics
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hyprctl (1)
.PP
The wiki at
.UR https://github.com/polarours/hyprfetch
.UE
